[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Handling in Spring Boot: Global and Controller-Specific"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is a crucial aspect of building robust and maintainable applications. Spring Boot provides powerful mechanisms for handling exceptions, allowing you to gracefully manage errors and provide informative responses to clients. This tutorial explores both global and controller-specific exception handling techniques in Spring Boot."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Introduction to Exception Handling"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In any application, exceptions are inevitable. They can arise from various sources, such as invalid user input, network errors, database issues, or unexpected runtime conditions. Without proper exception handling, these errors can lead to application crashes, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot simplifies exception handling by providing annotations and classes that allow you to define centralized error handling logic. You can handle exceptions at different levels, including globally for the entire application and specifically for individual controllers."},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Global Exception Handling with @ControllerAdvice"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@ControllerAdvice` annotation allows you to define a global exception handler that applies to all controllers in your application. This is useful for handling common exceptions, such as `NotFoundException`, `IllegalArgumentException`, or custom exceptions specific to your application."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2.1 Creating a Global Exception Handler"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a global exception handler, you need to create a class annotated with `@ControllerAdvice` and use the `@ExceptionHandler` annotation to define methods that handle specific exception types."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleResourceNotFoundException(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        ErrorResponse errorResponse = new ErrorResponse(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND.value(), ex.getMessage());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errorResponse, HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleIllegalArgumentException(IllegalArgumentException ex) {
        ErrorResponse errorResponse = new ErrorResponse(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value(), ex.getMessage());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errorResponse, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleGenericException(Exception ex) {
        ErrorResponse errorResponse = new ErrorResponse(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR.value(), "An unexpected error occurred.");
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errorResponse, HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
    }
}

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
class ErrorResponse {
    private int statusCode;
    private String message;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@ControllerAdvice` indicates that this class provides advice to all controllers."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)` specifies that the `handleResourceNotFoundException` method should handle `ResourceNotFoundException` exceptions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `handleResourceNotFoundException` method creates an `ErrorResponse` object containing the error message and HTTP status code (404 Not Found) and returns it in a `ResponseEntity`."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Similar handlers are defined for `IllegalArgumentException` and a generic `Exception`."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"2.2 Creating Custom Exceptions"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's often beneficial to create custom exception classes that represent specific error conditions in your application. This improves code readability and allows you to handle exceptions more precisely."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"public class ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public ResourceNotFoundException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"2.3 Using the Global Exception Handler"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, whenever a `ResourceNotFoundException` or `IllegalArgumentException` is thrown from any controller in your application, the corresponding handler method in `GlobalExceptionHandler` will be invoked, and the appropriate error response will be returned to the client."},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"3. Controller-Specific Exception Handling with @ExceptionHandler"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In addition to global exception handling, you can also define exception handlers within specific controllers using the `@ExceptionHandler` annotation. This allows you to handle exceptions that are specific to a particular controller's logic."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"3.1 Implementing Controller-Specific Exception Handling"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        // Simulate user retrieval
        if (id <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid user ID");
        }
        if (id > 100) {
            throw new ResourceNotFoundException("User not found with ID: " + id);
        }
        return new User(id, "User " + id);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleIllegalArgumentException(IllegalArgumentException ex) {
        ErrorResponse errorResponse = new ErrorResponse(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value(), ex.getMessage());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errorResponse, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleResourceNotFoundException(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        ErrorResponse errorResponse = new ErrorResponse(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND.value(), ex.getMessage());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errorResponse, HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

}

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
class User {
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `UserController` defines two exception handler methods: `handleIllegalArgumentException` and `handleResourceNotFoundException`."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"These handlers are specific to this controller and will only be invoked if the corresponding exceptions are thrown from within the `UserController`."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If a `ResourceNotFoundException` is thrown, and there's a global exception handler AND a controller-specific handler, the controller-specific handler takes precedence."},{"order":29,"type":"heading","text":"4. Exception Handling Hierarchy and Precedence"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"When an exception is thrown, Spring Boot follows a specific hierarchy to determine which exception handler to invoke:"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. **Controller-Specific Exception Handlers:** If an exception handler is defined within the controller that threw the exception, it will be invoked first."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. **Global Exception Handlers:** If no controller-specific handler is found, Spring Boot will search for a global exception handler (defined with `@ControllerAdvice`) that can handle the exception."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. **Default Error Handling:** If no suitable exception handler is found, Spring Boot's default error handling mechanism will be used, which typically returns a generic error page or a JSON response with an error message."},{"order":34,"type":"heading","text":"5. Best Practices for Exception Handling"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Be Specific:** Handle exceptions as specifically as possible. Catch only the exceptions you expect and can handle gracefully."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Provide Informative Error Messages:** Include relevant information in your error messages to help users understand the problem and how to resolve it."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Log Exceptions:** Log exceptions for debugging and monitoring purposes. Include the exception message, stack trace, and any relevant context information."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Avoid Catching and Ignoring Exceptions:** Don't catch exceptions and do nothing with them. This can mask underlying problems and make it difficult to debug your application."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Return Appropriate HTTP Status Codes:** Use appropriate HTTP status codes to indicate the type of error that occurred (e.g., 400 Bad Request, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error)."},{"order":40,"type":"heading","text":"6. Conclusion"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is an essential part of building robust and reliable Spring Boot applications. By using global and controller-specific exception handling techniques, you can effectively manage errors, provide informative responses to clients, and improve the overall quality of your application. Remember to follow best practices for exception handling to ensure that your application is easy to debug, maintain, and scale."}]
